    (v_topic_id, '365: A 2-month-old presents with sudden onset of high fever, productive cough, chest pain, and lobar consolidation on chest X-ray. What is the most likely bacterial cause?', '["Haemophilus influenzae", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Staphylococcus aureus"]', 2, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of typical, community-acquired bacterial pneumonia in children beyond the neonatal period. (Blueprints)'),
    (v_topic_id, '366: A school-aged child (2-month-old) presents with a gradual onset of low-grade fever, malaise, headache, and a persistent hacking cough. Chest X-ray shows diffuse, bilateral interstitial infiltrates. What is the most likely pathogen?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "Respiratory Syncytial Virus", "Klebsiella pneumoniae"]', 1, 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a leading cause of atypical ''''walking'''' pneumonia in school-aged children and adolescents. (Nelson Essentials)'),
    (v_topic_id, '367: An infant born via vaginal delivery develops a staccato cough, tachypnea, and peripheral eosinophilia at 4 weeks of age. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation and diffuse infiltrates. What is the diagnosis?', '["RSV bronchiolitis", "Pertussis", "Chlamydia trachomatis pneumonia", "Group B Streptococcus pneumonia"]', 2, 'Chlamydia trachomatis pneumonia typically presents between 3 to 12 weeks of age with a staccato cough, afebrile presentation, and eosinophilia. (BRS Pediatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, '368: What is the most common cause of pneumonia in neonates (birth to 28 days)?', '["Group B Streptococcus (GBS) and E. coli", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "Respiratory Syncytial Virus", "Streptococcus pneumoniae"]', 0, 'Neonatal pneumonia is most commonly caused by pathogens acquired from the maternal genital tract, including GBS, E. coli, and Listeria monocytogenes. (Blueprints)'),
    (v_topic_id, '369: A 2-month-old toddler with a history of meconium ileus presents with recurrent pulmonary infections and failure to thrive. A sweat chloride test is 85 mEq/L (Normal < 40). What is the underlying genetic defect?', '["Mutation in the CFTR gene (e.g., delta F508)", "Mutation in the dystrophin gene", "Mutation in the alpha-1 antitrypsin gene", "Defect in ciliary dynein arms"]', 0, 'Cystic fibrosis is caused by an autosomal recessive mutation in the CFTR gene, leading to defective chloride transport, thick secretions, and multi-organ dysfunction. (Nelson Essentials)'),
    (v_topic_id, '370: Which pathogen is the most common cause of severe, chronic lung infections and rapid decline in pulmonary function in older children and adults with Cystic Fibrosis?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Haemophilus influenzae", "Burkholderia cepacia"]', 1, 'While S. aureus is common in infancy, Pseudomonas aeruginosa eventually colonizes the lungs of most CF patients and is a major determinant of morbidity and mortality. (Blueprints)');
